{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":true,"decision":"pass","reasons":[],"slug":"mx-macro-data","displayName":"Global Macro Database Assistant","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/financial-ai-analyst/skills/mx-macro-data","publisherHandle":"financial-ai-analyst","publisherDisplayName":"Financial AI Analyst","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/financial-ai-analyst","version":"1.0.12","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1776424761300,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/mx-macro-data/card?ownerHandle=financial-ai-analyst&version=1.0.12","sha256":"306c6d33331bcc35b0d8e22d2c60d57a9df6d403defcd57ac0230f3db7c865b2","size":2639,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"1b6b344c3cdd45ed08d9b530e0ae5aef58424e582a9b3eccca7b7bef1bd22c4e","bundleFingerprints":["4d7f96d43d2b002519ade7924bb4e287255b0ecddd143e76aaa044200a53542c","eccafe0fb0adf72031de5d23c2bab27fad7bc0d4576b7f8f402aab290ac98aee"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":13171,"sha256":"2a86e1520737b927ba488f81ace80420f894abc7ebf64df46a563dc5f4843497","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/get_data.py","size":19269,"sha256":"cd3fbc80220df8eb80b95e17395c3eb8f01b40c5eaa1519b1f1956eca1ad2423","contentType":"text/plain"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"clean","passed":true,"rawStatus":"clean","verdict":"benign","confidence":"high","summary":"This skill appears to do what it says: query Eastmoney macroeconomic data, then save CSV and description files, with some install and CSV-handling cautions.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789173036649,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Spreadsheet Formula Injection in Generated CSV Files"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"scripts/get_data.py"},"region":{"startLine":269,"endLine":281}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"fb04d6f13667025b"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `scripts/get_data.py:269-281`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Spreadsheet formula injection through untrusted API values  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\n```python\n# Generate the file name\ncsv_path = output_dir / f\"mx_macro_data_{unique_suffix}_{frequency}.csv\"\n\n# Write CSV\nwith open(csv_path, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8-sig\") as f:\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(\n        f,\n        fieldnames=fieldnames,\n        extrasaction=\"ignore\",\n    )\n    writer.writeheader()\n    for row in rows:\n        writer.writerow({k: _flatten_value(v) for k, v in row.items()})\n```\n\nThe value-conversion function used before serialization does not apply spreadsheet-specific escaping:\n\n```python\ndef _flatten_value(v: Any) -> str:\n    if v is None:\n        return \"\"\n    if isinstance(v, (dict, list)):\n        return json.dumps(v, ensure_ascii=False)\n    return str(v)\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nValues returned by the remote macroeconomic-data API are converted to strings and written directly into CSV cells. No validation or neutralization is applied to strings beginning with spreadsheet formula markers such as `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@`.\n\nAlthough CSV is nominally a data format, common spreadsheet applications may interpret cells beginning with these characters as formulas. Consequently, a malicious or compromised API response could place an attacker-controlled formula in a generated file. The formula may be evaluated when the user opens the CSV in a compatible spreadsheet application.\n\nThe vulnerability crosses a trust boundary because remote response content is treated as safe spreadsheet data without output encoding appropriate to the eventual consumer.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An attacker compromises the upstream API, influences a data source consumed by it, or otherwise causes a table value to contain a spreadsheet formula.\n2. The API returns a value such as `=HYPERLINK(\"https://attacker.example/...\",\"Open\")` or another application-supported formula.\n3. `_flatten_value()` preserves the formula marker and returns the value unchanged.\n4. `_write_csv_file()` writes the value directly into a CSV cell.\n5. A user opens the generated CSV in a spreadsheet application.\n6. The application interprets the cell as a formula.\n7. Depending on the application and its security settings, the formula may initiate an external request, disclose data embedded in the formula, mislead the user, or invoke other spreadsheet-supported behavior.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe direct impact is limited to users who open the generated CSV in software that evaluates formulas. Exploitation does not grant privileges through the Python process itself. Instead, the payload executes within the context and security model of the spreadsheet application under the current user's account.\n\nPotential effects include:\n\n- External network requests that reveal identifiers or confirm that the file was opened.\n- Deceptive links or content used for phishing and social engineering.\n- Disclosure of spreadsheet-accessible data where supported by formulas.\n- More serious application-specific behavior if the spreadsheet environment enables unsafe legacy features or external integrations.\n\nThe exact scope depends on the spreadsheet product, operating-system integration, and security settings.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\nApply spreadsheet-safe encoding to every remotely sourced value before writing it to CSV.\n\n1. Introduce a dedicated function that neutralizes formula-like strings:\n\n```python\ndef _escape_spreadsheet_value(value: Any) -> str:\n    text = _flatten_value(value)\n    if text.startswith((\"=\", \"+\", \"-\", \"@\")):\n        return \"'\" + text\n    return text\n```\n\n2. Use the function for all cells:\n\n```python\nwriter.writerow({\n    key: _escape_spreadsheet_value(value)\n    for key, value in row.items()\n})\n```\n\n3. Consider handling leading tabs, carriage returns, newlines, and whitespace before a formula marker because spreadsheet behavior varies by product.\n\n4. Add automated tests covering values beginning with `=`, `+`, `-`, and `@`, as well as ordinary negative numbers if preserving their numeric type is important.\n\n5. Document that exported files contain untrusted external data and recommend opening them with external-content and macro execution disabled.\n\n6. If consumers require machine-readable numeric values, consider a safer structured format such as JSON or provide a strict CSV mode that validates values against the expected schema and data types.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T08","level":"note","message":{"text":"Unpinned Third-Party Dependency Installation"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":10,"endLine":16}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"413f0103a5835eba"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md:10-16` and `SKILL.md:169-174`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned dependency and non-reproducible package installation  \n**Risk Level**: Low\n\n### Vulnerable Configuration and Command\n\nThe Skill metadata requests installation of `httpx` without a version constraint:\n\n```json\n\"install\": [\n  {\n    \"id\": \"pip-deps\",\n    \"kind\": \"python\",\n    \"package\": \"httpx\",\n    \"label\": \"Install Python dependencies\"\n  }\n]\n```\n\nThe manual installation instructions also install the latest version available at resolution time:\n\n```bash\npip3 install httpx --user\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe project depends on the third-party `httpx` package but does not pin an audited version, provide a lock file, or require package-integrity hashes. As a result, identical installations performed at different times may resolve to different package versions and dependency trees.\n\nAn unpinned package is not inherently malicious, and the reviewed project does not use a misspelled or obviously deceptive package name. However, unrestricted dependency resolution expands supply-chain exposure. A compromised upstream release, a newly introduced vulnerability, or a backward-incompatible update could be installed automatically without a corresponding review of the Skill.\n\nThe use of `--user` installs the package into the invoking user's Python environment. This can affect other Python applications that share that environment and allows package installation or import-time code to execute with the user's privileges.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. The Skill is installed or a user follows the documented installation command.\n2. `pip` resolves `httpx` and its transitive dependencies using the package versions available at that time.\n3. If an upstream package release or dependency has been compromised, the affected package is downloaded without a project-defined version or hash check.\n4. Installation hooks, package imports, or subsequent Skill execution run the compromised code under the invoking user's account.\n5. The malicious dependency can access resources available to that account, including environment variables, local files, and network connectivity, subject to host controls.\n\nThis path depends on compromise or unsafe changes in the upstream dependency supply chain; no such compromise was observed during this static audit.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nIf the dependency supply chain were compromised, code could execute with the privileges of the user installing or running the Skill. Potential scope includes:\n\n- Reading files accessible to the current user.\n- Accessing environment variables available to the Python process, including `EM_API_KEY`.\n- Making outbound network requests.\n- Modifying files in locations writable by the user.\n- Affecting other Python programs that use the same user-level environment.\n\nNo privilege escalation beyond the invoking user's existing permissions is demonstrated by the reviewed code.\n]]>","severity":"Low"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Pin `httpx` to a specifically reviewed version or a narrowly controlled compatible range:\n\n```bash\npip3 install \"httpx==<reviewed-version>\"\n```\n\n2. Declare dependencies in a standard requirements or lock file rather than relying only on prose installation instructions.\n\n3. Generate and verify cryptographic hashes, for example by using a hash-locked requirements file and installing with:\n\n```bash\npip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt\n```\n\n4. Pin and audit transitive dependencies as well as the direct `httpx` dependency.\n\n5. Install the Skill in an isolated virtual environment instead of the shared user-level Python environment.\n\n6. Use a trusted package index over TLS and explicitly control any additional package-index configuration.\n\n7. Establish a dependency-update process that includes vulnerability scanning, changelog review, compatibility testing, and deliberate lock-file regeneration.\n]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":65,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789172928.2428048,"endTime":1789173002.592842}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"mx-macro-data","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57708pg4jszpbw1x1b0gdfz2s8e7kp2-0tU7Sg/clawscan-skillspector-3456388196/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-12T00:28:47.174176+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":24,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","max_issue_severity":"MEDIUM"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":274,"executable":false,"size_bytes":13171,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"scripts/get_data.py","type":"python","lines":507,"executable":true,"size_bytes":19269,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"LP3","finding_id":"finding-099305dc54f1473696ebc7ba4260ba5e","category":"MCP Least Privilege","pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.94,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The skill declares capabilities that use environment secrets, filesystem writes, and outbound network access, but it does not explicitly constrain tool scope via permissions or allowed-tools metadata. In an agent platform, that mismatch can lead to overbroad execution privileges, making accidental secret exposure, unintended file modification, or unexpected external requests more likely if the skill or surrounding orchestrator is misused.","remediation":"Add explicit least-privilege tool scoping to the skill manifest, limiting it to only the exact tools required for HTTP requests, reading the EM_API_KEY, and writing output files in a designated directory. Also document and enforce path restrictions, redact secrets from logs/outputs, and deny any unnecessary shell or general-purpose execution capabilities.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-3","finding_id":"finding-9757e8e0873347fd80d5efc64be4581a","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.89,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":22,"end_line":24},"finding":null,"explanation":"The user-facing title and primary operating instructions are presented in Chinese, and the skill describes interaction via text input without stating that other languages are supported. Under the policy, forcing a specific language without user opt-in is a natural-language policy concern unless a locale constraint is clearly documented and justified.","remediation":"State explicitly whether the skill supports multiple languages, or document that Chinese is required and why. Preferably add a user-facing note offering language choice or clarifying accepted query languages.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":22,"end_line":24}]},{"id":"SQP-3","finding_id":"finding-e11db48e99fa41e5ac38c91331f5c203","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"scripts/get_data.py","start_line":2,"end_line":4},"finding":null,"explanation":"The module docstring and user-facing behavior describe the skill exclusively in Chinese and state that users can query via text input, but there is no indication that language is optional or configurable. This creates a natural-language policy concern because the skill effectively assumes a fixed language/locale without offering user choice or documenting a justified locale restriction.","remediation":"Document that the skill is Chinese-only if that restriction is intentional and justified, or add explicit language selection/opt-in handling in the CLI and user-facing documentation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"scripts/get_data.py","start_line":2,"end_line":4}]}],"suppressed_count":0,"suppressed":[],"metadata":{"has_executable_scripts":true,"skillspector_version":"2.11.2","llm_requested":true,"llm_available":true,"meta_analysis_applied":true,"inference_usage":[{"node":"mcp_tool_poisoning","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage_source":"provider_response","prompt_tokens":4653,"completion_tokens":328,"cached_tokens":0,"reasoning_tokens":0,"total_tokens":4981},{"node":"semantic_developer_intent","request_kind":"structured_output","provider":"openai","model":"gpt-5.4-2026-03-05","model_source":"provider_response","usage_source":"provider_response","prompt_tokens":7251,"completion_tokens":14,"cached_tokens"